# .gitignore ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Description:
#   Repository-wide exclusions for generated build products, simulation data,
#   local run state, interpreter caches, and editor/operating-system artifacts.
# Purpose:
#   Keep reproducible source and configuration under version control while
#   preventing large, machine-specific, or regenerable files from being committed.
# Workflow:
#   Git evaluates these patterns from the repository root when reporting,
#   staging, or cleaning files. Protected external and legacy sources remain
#   tracked unless one of their generated byproducts matches a rule below.
# Inputs:
#   Files produced by CMake, ROOT, GEMC, reconstruction, Python, editors, and
#   local workflow configuration.
# Outputs:
#   A clean working tree containing maintained source rather than generated data.

# Compiled and ROOT-generated artifacts ---------------------------------------
# ROOT data and C++/ROOT build byproducts are reproducible from maintained input.
*.root
*.so
*.pcm
*.d

# Simulation and batch-job outputs -------------------------------------------
# GEMC event data, reconstructed HIPO files, and captured job output can be large.
*.ev
*.hipo
*.out

# Editor and operating-system artifacts --------------------------------------
# Ignore common backup files, Python bytecode caches, and macOS metadata.
*~
*#
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.DS_Store

# Generated working directories ----------------------------------------------
# CMake build trees, temporary workspaces, and completed local runs are disposable.
build/
tmp/
/runs/

# Historical generated converter artifact ------------------------------------
# Retain the legacy path-specific exclusion for an ACLiC dependency file.
GEMC-samples/GENIE_to_LUND_converter/GENIE_to_LUND_converter_C.d

# Machine-local CMake and workflow configuration ------------------------------
# User presets and local run overrides must not replace reviewed shared defaults.
/CMakeUserPresets.json
/config/run.local.json
